  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 1158 PM CDT Thu Oct 8 2026\n\nSaturday through Thursday:\n\nThe gusty south winds will continue into Saturday, within a \ntight pressure gradient. This should bring very warm \ntemperatures into the area, with 925 mb temperatures rising to\n20 to 21 degrees Celsius. Highs may reach to around 80 degrees \nor into the lower 80s. Record highs are 84 degrees for \nMilwaukee and Madison, so those may be approached. \n\nOne thing to watch will be perhaps a south southeast lake \nbreeze developing for areas near the lakeshore by midday and \ninto the afternoon. This may hold down highs a bit in those \nareas. Also, the clouds associated with the remnants of the \ntropical cyclone may push into southeast Wisconsin Saturday \nafternoon, which may hold down highs a bit there.\n\nAny rain with the tropical cyclone should remain southeast of \nthe area Saturday night into Sunday. There will be clouds from \nthis system that linger Saturday night into early Sunday in \nsoutheast parts of the area. Sunday still looks like a warm day,\nwith highs well into the 70s inland. Onshore winds should keep \neastern areas in the upper 60s to lower 70s.\n\nThere still appears to be a cold front that shifts southeast\nthrough the area Monday evening. Ensemble members are fairly \nwet Monday night, so kept 20 to 50 percent chances for showers \ngoing. Some thunderstorms may occur as well, as the airmass may \nbe somewhat unstable ahead of the front. \n\nHigh pressure behind this system with some cold air advection \nshould bring more seasonable temperatures by the middle of next \nweek. A more organized low pressure system moving northeast \nthrough the region later in the week may bring a better chance \nfor showers and perhaps some storms.\n\nWood",
